/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The in-line jerk pump, called a plunger pump in some syllabi, has one pumping element per cylinder driven by one camshaft.
   - Each element is a plunger inside a barrel.
   - Effective stroke seff is the plunger travel from port closing to spill opening.
   - V is the fuel volume per injection (m³); d is the plunger diameter (m); seff is the effective stroke (m).
   - The control rack rotates the plunger to change fuel quantity.
   - The distributor pump, or rotary pump, uses one pumping element for all cylinders and a rotating distributor that sends each charge to the next cylinder in firing order.
   - The unit injector puts the pump and nozzle in one body at the cylinder head.
   - The plunger pressurises fuel just above the nozzle needle.
   - The short, stiff path gives fast pressure rise, precise timing and high pressure.
   - All three need timing.
*/
