/* book exam traps: Using total plunger stroke instead of effective stroke. The effective stroke starts only after the inlet and spill ports are covered by the plunger. | Thinking that rotating the plunger changes its stroke length. It changes the angular position of the helix, so the spill port opens earlier or later. | Confusing the delivery valve with the injector nozzle. The delivery valve is at the pump outlet and holds residual line pressure; the nozzle atomises fuel in the chamber. | Assuming a distributor pump feeds all cylinders at the same instant. The rotor lines up with one delivery union at a time in firing order. | Forgetting that a unit injector still needs timing. The cam lobe, rocker clearance or shims set its timing even though there is no high-pressure pipe. */
