/* book: lead: A common rail stores diesel at high pressure in one pipe and lets an electronic control unit fire each injector when it wants. The high-pressure pump now feeds a rail continuously, so pressure generation is separated from the injection event. Rail pressure and injection timing become independent settings. This gives fine sprays, quiet combustion and low emissions. It is used in cars, buses, trucks */
/* book Figure 2.4.1: A four-cylinder common rail. The pump keeps the rail at the pressure the ECU sets, the sensor reports it, and the ECU opens each injector for a time: 1.0 ms gives 30 mm³ at 1200 bar, but at 1800 bar the same fuel needs only 0.81 ms, as the second worked example shows. */
